"Dosimetry: Kerma, Equilibrium and the Ion Chamber: teaching notes",
"Dosimetry: Kerma, Equilibrium and the Ion Chamber",
"Upper level medical physics track (juniors in radiation physics, seniors before the proton center lab); calculus; Attix, Introduction to Radiological Physics and Radiation Dosimetry, chapters {#} to {#} and {#} to {#}, and IAEA TRS-{#}",
"Kerma is energy handed to charged particles, dose is energy left behind. The electrons carry the energy forward, so near the surface the dose builds up from almost nothing while the collision kerma is already falling.",
"Past the farthest electron range, dose and collision kerma fall together as e^(−μz) with a fixed ratio β = D/K_col slightly above {#}: transient charged particle equilibrium. The depth of the dose maximum tracks the range of the fastest electrons, which is why megavoltage beams spare the skin.",
"A Monte Carlo estimate carries a statistical uncertainty that shrinks as {#}/√N; agreement with a model is judged against it.",
"An ion chamber measures charge, not dose: D_air = (Q/m)(W_air/e), then Bragg–Gray gives D_w = D_air s_w,air p_Q. Recombination, polarity and air density all change the charge, and each has its correction.",
"TRS-{#} turns this into a recipe: D_w,Q = M k_TP k_pol k_s N_D,w,Q0 k_Q,Q0, where N_D,w comes from a ⁶⁰Co standards laboratory and k_Q carries the stopping power ratios and W_air from ⁶⁰Co to the user's beam.",
"The strip switches between three views. <strong>Kerma and dose</strong> sends a broad beam of photons of one energy ({#} to {#} MeV) into water: the picture shows interactions and the Compton electrons they start, to scale; the graph shows the collision kerma (NIST μ_en/ρ), the model's dose and Monte Carlo points with their uncertainties, and a depth probe. <strong>Ion chamber</strong> is a {#} cm³ Farmer chamber cut along its axis, with the saturation curve or the Jaffé plot ({#}/M against {#}/V or {#}/V²) for a pulsed proton beam or continuous ⁶⁰Co. <strong>Calibration</strong> is a water phantom with the chamber at the reference depth, an electrometer, a thermometer in the water and a barometer, for a scanned proton layer ({#} to {#} MeV) or ⁶⁰Co at a standards laboratory, with the calibration steps and a Check for each factor in the sidebar. Mystery machine gives the proton beam a drifted output and a new room. Blind hides the model's dose, β, d_max, k_s and the checks.",
"<strong>⁶⁰Co buildup</strong>: start it and let a million histories run. Ask Q1 while the Monte Carlo points fill in under the model curve.",
"<strong>Equilibrium at depth</strong>: ask Q2. Then <strong>{#} MeV: a thin buildup</strong> and <strong>{#} MeV: a deep buildup</strong> for Q3.",
