"<strong>Where N_D,w comes from</strong>, then <strong>Calibrate a {#} MeV layer</strong>: work down the steps together. Ask Q5 and Q6.",
"Turn on Mystery machine (a simulated output drift of {#} to {#} %, either way, and a new room) and let groups find the drift and decide whether it is significant.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Why is the dose at the surface nearly zero when the photons' collision kerma is largest there? Where does the dose reach its maximum, and what sets that depth?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> Past the buildup, D/K_col is constant and a little above {#}. How can the dose exceed the collision kerma without creating energy?",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> For {#}, {#} and {#} MeV the model's d_max is about {#}, {#} and {#} mm. Relate each to the fastest Compton electron. Why do megavoltage beams spare the skin?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> In a pulsed beam, {#}/M is nearly a straight line in {#}/V; in ⁶⁰Co the reading hardly changes from {#} V to {#} V. Why does a pulsed beam lose more ions to recombination than a continuous beam of the same average dose rate, and why does k_s grow with the dose per pulse?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> At {#} MeV the PTW {#} chamber's k_Q is {#}. Where does each part of k_Q = (s_w,air W_air)_Q/((s_w,air W_air)_Co p_Co) come from, and which part makes it larger than {#}?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> The room is at {#} °C and {#} kPa. Compute k_TP. If the physicist forgot it, by how much would the beam be miscalibrated, and in which direction would every patient's dose then go?",
"<strong>\"Kerma and dose are the same thing.\"</strong> They are equal only under charged particle equilibrium (and without radiative losses). In the buildup the dose is lower; past it, slightly higher.",
"<strong>\"The dose maximum is where most photons interact.\"</strong> Most interactions are at the surface. The maximum is where electrons from upstream have built up.",
"<strong>\"An ion chamber measures dose directly.\"</strong> It measures charge in a small mass of air; dose to water needs W_air, a stopping power ratio and several corrections.",
"<strong>\"A higher voltage always gives a bigger reading.\"</strong> Only until saturation; past that the reading is flat (and at very high voltage charge multiplication would start, which the demo does not model).",
